      "answer": {
        "answer_markdown": "### (a) Labor force and participation rate\n\nLabor force = employed + unemployed actively seeking work:\n\n\\[\n320{,}000+20{,}000=340{,}000\n\\]\n\nLabor-force participation rate = labor force ÷ civilian noninstitutional adult population × 100:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{340{,}000}{500{,}000}\\times100=68\\%\n\\]\n\n### (b) Actual unemployment rate\n\n\\[\n\\frac{20{,}000\\text{ unemployed}}{340{,}000\\text{ labor force}}\\times100\\approx5.88\\%\\quad(\\text{about }5.9\\%)\n\\]\n\n### (c) After 5,000 unemployed adults stop looking\n\nThey are no longer counted as unemployed or as part of the labor force. The new unemployed total is 15,000, and the new labor force is 335,000:\n\n\\[\n\\text{New unemployment rate}=\\frac{15{,}000}{335{,}000}\\times100\\approx4.48\\%\\quad(\\text{about }4.5\\%)\n\\]\n\nThe measured rate falls because people who stop actively searching are classified as outside the labor force, not unemployed. This decline does not mean that more people found jobs; employment remains 320,000.\n\n### (d) Production possibilities curve before part (c)\n\nA point inside the PPC is consistent with recessionary unemployment because resources are underused. The natural unemployment rate does not imply that output must be at the PPC.",
